    "result_text": "Green. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec's \"allow\" behaviour was already present in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`. The only missing pieces were **assigning** and **returning** a stable, shared `householdId`. I implemented it deterministically so joiners in the same household resolve to the same id without any write-back to existing owners.\n\nChanges (3 files, all new/tiny cohesive units \u2014 nothing added to the large controller class):\n\n1. **`model/HouseholdId.java`** (new) \u2014 a small final utility (mirroring `CustomerCode`/`PhoneNumbers`) whose `of(lastName, address)` normalizes the household key (trim, collapse whitespace, lowercase \u2014 matching the controller's existing `sameHousehold` semantics) and returns a stable 12-char SHA-256 hex id.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 one `@Transient` computed getter `getHouseholdId()` delegating to `HouseholdId.of(...)`. Because it's derived, any two owners with the same last name + address (the household-sharing pair) always report the same id, and it survives the separate `GET` regardless of persistence.\n\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` as a `readOnly` string on the `Owner` schema, so the generated DTO carries it into responses; MapStruct auto-maps `getHouseholdId()` \u2192 DTO.\n\nNo DB schema change was needed (the field is computed, not stored), keeping structural impact low. The new `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` and the full suite pass.",
